Speckit Constitution

Purpose
Define the shared principles for this project so quality, tests, UX, and
performance are consistent and measurable.

Principles

1) Code Quality
- Prioritize clarity over cleverness; prefer explicit intent and readable flows.
- Keep modules focused (single responsibility) and avoid deep coupling.
- Use meaningful names, small functions, and early returns to reduce nesting.
- Remove dead code and avoid unused dependencies.
- Document non-obvious decisions and tradeoffs at the point of change.

2) Testing Standards
- Write tests for new behavior and bug fixes; no regressions without a test.
- Cover core logic with unit tests and critical flows with integration tests.
- Validate error paths and edge cases, not only happy paths.
- Keep tests deterministic: no reliance on real time, network, or global state.
- Tests must be easy to run locally and in CI with clear failure output.

3) User Experience Consistency
- Align UI copy, spacing, and component behavior with existing patterns.
- Ensure accessibility basics: keyboard navigation, focus states, and contrast.
- Prefer consistent loading, empty, and error states across pages.
- Avoid UI drift by reusing shared components and tokens when available.
- Changes should not degrade responsiveness on common breakpoints.

4) Performance Requirements
- Measure before optimizing; avoid speculative micro-optimizations.
- Keep critical paths fast: reduce blocking JS, excessive re-renders, and N+1
  data access.
- Use lazy loading and caching where appropriate; define cache invalidation.
- Guard against large payloads and unbounded lists; paginate or virtualize.
- Maintain baseline performance budgets and track regressions.
